WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
He may not be as famous as J. Robert Oppenheimer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.560
but Enrico Fermi’s experiments are what&nbsp;
led to the creation of the atomic bomb.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.880
Growing up in Rome, his brightness caught&nbsp;
the attention of his father’s colleague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.200
railway engineer Adolfo Amidei who&nbsp;
guided him and taught him far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.780
about math and physics than he&nbsp;
was learning in high school.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.340
Had he not nurtured the 13-year-old Enrico,&nbsp;
history might have turned out differently.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:29.280
By the time Enrico entered university in 1918&nbsp;
to study physics at the prestigious Scuola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.440
Normale Superiore in Pisa, there really wasn’t&nbsp;
anything more his professors could teach him. 
&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.420
So he spent a lot of time studying&nbsp;
quantum physics and relativity on his own.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:44.160
He was thoroughly impressed with Einstein’s&nbsp;
Special Theory of Relativity, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.400
his equation E = mc2, which shows that mass&nbsp;
and energy are different forms of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.780
thing…and that a small amount of mass can&nbsp;
be converted into a large amount of energy.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.520
As a student, he wrote: “If we could&nbsp;
liberate the energy in one gram of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.840
matter we would get more energy than&nbsp;
exerted by a thousand horses working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.480
continuously over three years…an explosion&nbsp;
of such an awesome amount of energy would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.520
blow to pieces the physicist who had the&nbsp;
misfortune of finding a way to produce it.”

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.740
These words were prophetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.420
given his later work developing the&nbsp;
deadliest weapon known to humankind.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.860
After studying in Pisa, he went off&nbsp;
to the University of Gottingen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.040
Germany, where the Italian was&nbsp;
overlooked by his counterparts.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.240
Marie Curie apparently “...ignored him&nbsp;
to the point of exceeding rudeness,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.140
according to a future colleague of his on&nbsp;
the Manhattan Project to build the bomb.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.460
As he had done in Pisa, he focused&nbsp;
on probability theory in Germany.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.500
His mastery of probability allowed him to&nbsp;
make accurate predictions with minimal data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.040
a vital piece in figuring out&nbsp;
how to create the atomic bomb.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.020
According to biographer David Schwartz,&nbsp;
Fermi’s interest in probability theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.560
may have been sparked by the death&nbsp;
of his beloved brother Giulio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.060
who died during an operation on a&nbsp;
throat abscess at just 15 years old.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.520
Schwartz surmises, “Fermi may have taken away&nbsp;
from this trauma the need to understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.920
likelihood of any particular event and a feeling&nbsp;
that in understanding that probability he was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.600
a better position to anticipate it, prepare&nbsp;
for it, and perhaps even shape its outcome.”

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.900
His set of guidelines called&nbsp;
Fermi-Dirac statistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.740
developed in tandem with English&nbsp;
physicist Paul Dirac, rely on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.080
use of probabilistic reasoning to predict the&nbsp;
energy levels of particles called fermions.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.760
Imagine particles are concertgoers,&nbsp;
and their energy levels are seats.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.740
Just as each person has a unique seat,&nbsp;
each particle has a unique energy level.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:46.020
Just as no two people can share a seat, no&nbsp;
two particles can share the same energy level.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.000
His development of Fermi-Dirac statistics&nbsp;
propelled him from his job teaching at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.020
University of Florence to a prestigious&nbsp;
position at the University of Rome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.560
where he became Italy’s first&nbsp;
professor of theoretical physics.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.520
He sought to establish Rome as&nbsp;
a leading center of physics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.740
was a natural leader others gravitated toward.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:09.120
In the Italian capital, he developed&nbsp;
his explanation of beta decay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.220
a process where unstable atoms break down&nbsp;
and emit a specific type of radiation.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.520
Fermi proposed that inside these decaying atoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.060
one of the neutrons is turning&nbsp;
into a proton. When this happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.700
the neutron releases an electron and a tiny,&nbsp;
almost massless particle called a neutrino.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.020
Although this theory solidified his&nbsp;
reputation as a leading physicist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:36.840
it was his later experiments developing the atomic&nbsp;
bomb that catapulted him into the limelight.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.340
He built on the work of Marie Curie and&nbsp;
her husband, Pierre, who discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.620
bombarding substances with tiny particles called&nbsp;
alpha particles could make them radioactive.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:52.560
Inspired by the Curies, Fermi did something&nbsp;
similar using neutrons and discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.280
certain elements bombarded with&nbsp;
neutrons also become radioactive.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.920
Fermi thought he had created new elements.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.420
He even won the Nobel Prize in&nbsp;
Physics in 1938 for this discovery.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.900
But what he had actually&nbsp;
done, was split uranium atoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.200
a process that is fundamental to&nbsp;
the operation of an atomic bomb.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.500
He didn’t realize it because&nbsp;
he thought the nucleus of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.820
uranium atom was like a solid brick&nbsp;
wall, incapable of changing shape.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.660
It wasn’t until German physicists&nbsp;
replicated his experiments five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:26.580
years later that they made a monumental&nbsp;
discovery; they realized firing neutrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.320
at uranium splits the uranium atom,&nbsp;
a process called nuclear fission.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.720
This releases an enormous amount of energy,&nbsp;
which is fundamental to nuclear weapons.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.420
Fermi found out about his embarrassing&nbsp;
overlook after he moved to America in 1939.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.600
Fascist Italy had become intolerable by then.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.520
Fermi had actually joined the Fascist party&nbsp;
earlier as it was common for career advancement.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.600
However, as Hitler pressed&nbsp;
Mussolini to target Italian Jews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.040
there was fear that his wife, Laura,&nbsp;
who was Jewish, would be persecuted.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.800
Another incentive to leave his homeland was that&nbsp;
the physicist and politician Orso Mario Corbino,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.540
who had guided his career and opened doors&nbsp;
for him, had died suddenly from pneumonia.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.380
After Corbino’s death, funding&nbsp;
for his research began to dry up.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.700
Fermi took the opportunity of collecting&nbsp;
his Nobel prize at the Awards ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:22.140
in Stockholm to the quietly head to New&nbsp;
York with his wife and two young children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.020
where a position awaited&nbsp;
him at Columbia University.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:30.120
Two weeks after arriving in&nbsp;
America on January 2, 1939,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.500
he learned about the major discovery of&nbsp;
nuclear fission that he should have made.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.620
But there was no time to&nbsp;
dwell on missed opportunities.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.860
Besides, the process of fission&nbsp;
was not fully understood.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.040
He joined forces with another&nbsp;
recent American immigrant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:50.280
Hungarian physicist Leo Szilard to understand and&nbsp;
control each step of the chain reaction process.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.440
They aimed to harness the enormous amount of&nbsp;
energy liberated within a fraction of a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.040
opening up the possibility that&nbsp;
uranium could be used for power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.520
generation or, potentially, to build a bomb.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.780
They were aware of the implications of&nbsp;
their work in light of the escalating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:11.040
tensions that would eventually lead&nbsp;
to WWII and informed the U.S. Navy.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.720
The dean of graduate studies at Columbia,&nbsp;
George Pegram sent a letter to Admiral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.980
Stanford Hooper outlining the potential&nbsp;
consequences of Fermi’s research:

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:24.960
“...this might mean the possibility that&nbsp;
uranium might be used as an explosive that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.160
would liberate a million times as&nbsp;
much energy per pound as any known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.880
explosive. My own feeling is that&nbsp;
the probabilities are against this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.080
but my colleagues and I think the bare&nbsp;
possibility should not be disregarded…”

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:41.760
The U.S. Navy gave Fermi a small&nbsp;
$1,500 grant to continue his work.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.460
The government’s interest in Fermi’s research only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.140
intensified after Szilard wrote a letter&nbsp;
to President Roosevelt clearly spelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.700
out that “extremely powerful bombs of&nbsp;
a new type may thus be constructed”.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:58.560
He warned that Nazy Germany was demonstrating&nbsp;
a keen interest in uranium research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.320
hinting at their pursuit of a nuclear weapon.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.680
The most famous scientist&nbsp;
in exile, Albert Einstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.780
signed the letter to give it extra clout.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:12.420
The U.S. government would eventually spend&nbsp;
$2.2 billion to build the first atomic bomb.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.500
Scientists had to figure out how much&nbsp;
uranium would be needed to create a bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.160
and how to assemble the bomb in such&nbsp;
a way that it would explode properly.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.780
J. Robert Oppenheimer oversaw the&nbsp;
Manhattan Project, coordinating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.660
many teams who worked on different&nbsp;
aspects of the bomb’s development.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:33.300
The Manhattan Project was so secretive that most&nbsp;
staff had no idea what they were working on.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.100
Fermi was primarily responsible for&nbsp;
developing the first nuclear reactor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.540
which demonstrated that a nuclear chain&nbsp;
reaction could be initiated and controlled.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.940
By then, he had moved from Columbia to the&nbsp;
University of Chicago at the request of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:52.320
U.S. government, which wanted to consolidate all&nbsp;
the nuclear research projects across the country.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.260
On December 2, 1942, he and his team&nbsp;
conducted a nuclear experiment beneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.160
the University of Chicago's abandoned&nbsp;
football field, in the squash court area.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.300
It was the only space with high enough&nbsp;
ceilings to allow the construction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.340
the pile of bricks of graphite and uranium,&nbsp;
carefully arranged to facilitate a reaction.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:16.140
The graphite slowed down the speed of the neutrons&nbsp;
so that they could cause further fissions.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.560
This atomic furnace, called “Chicago Pile-1”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:25.080
demonstrated the first self-sustained chain&nbsp;
reaction, ushering in the nuclear age.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.880
When Fermi realized the reaction&nbsp;
was self-sustaining, “...his whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.920
face broke into a broad smile,” described&nbsp;
David Schwartz in his biography on Fermi.&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:35.820
tephen
A member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.200
of Fermi’s team recorded the moment in&nbsp;
a logbook, writing: “We’re cookin’!”.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.760
The team shared a bottle of wine to mark the&nbsp;
occasion, but they didn’t make any toasts.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:49.920
This wasn’t really a celebratory moment, as they&nbsp;
knew the implications of what they had achieved. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.860
The Chicago “pile” was the predecessor to the&nbsp;
giant nuclear reactors at Hanford, Washington.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.900
The U.S. government acknowledges Fermi’s&nbsp;
pivotal role in creating the bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.600
declaring, “More than any individual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.820
he was responsible for developing a means for&nbsp;
the controlled release of nuclear energy.”

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.480
Yet, Oppenheimer has gotten&nbsp;
far more credit than Fermi.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.480
One reason is that Fermi rarely appeared on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.820
and he wasn’t into self-promotion. And he also&nbsp;
passed away before television became widespread.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.780
On the other hand, Oppenheimer was&nbsp;
often interviewed and photographed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:26.400
as the leader of the Manhattan Project.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:31.020
Oppenheimer recognized and acknowledged&nbsp;
Fermi’s contributions and intellect.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.360
At a dinner party after Fermi’s death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.860
Manhattan Project physicist Leona Libby&nbsp;
recalled that Oppenheimer suggested a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:42.900
game he called “Who do you want to be on&nbsp;
your day off?” and then, he chose Fermi.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.320
In 1944, Fermi moved to Los Alamos, New Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.340
the main hub for the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;
where he became an associate director.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:55.560
He served as an advisor, offering advice to&nbsp;
other scientists when they ran into problems.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:02.760
On July 16, 1945, scientists detonated the first&nbsp;
nuclear device near Alamogordo, New Mexico.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:07.020
Fermi made a rough calculation of&nbsp;
its explosive energy by dropping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:10.860
small pieces of paper from his hand as the&nbsp;
shockwave arrived and estimated from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:15.840
deflection that the test had released&nbsp;
energy equivalent to 10,000 tons of TNT.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:20.400
The actual result was more than&nbsp;
twice that amount, 21,000 tons.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.780
This is enough energy to power two&nbsp;
million American homes for a year.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:29.340
The Trinity device unleashed at&nbsp;
Alamagordo had a plutonium core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.520
which Fermi and his team achieved&nbsp;
through a series of transformations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.120
with uranium that resulted in the&nbsp;
creation of the human-made element.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:42.060
This design was later replicated in one of&nbsp;
the two devastating bombs dropped on Japan.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:48.180
We don’t know how Fermi reacted to the bombing&nbsp;
of Hiroshima and Nagasaki and the hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:53.520
thousands of people who perished instantly or who&nbsp;
later died from injuries and radiation sickness.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.860
He kept his emotions to himself.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.220
What we can glean is that during&nbsp;
the development of the bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:04.020
Oppenheimer recalled Fermi was surprised&nbsp;
by the group’s enthusiasm for building such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:08.220
a weapon and remarked, “I believe our&nbsp;
people actually want to make a bomb.”

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:13.140
Fermi’s sister Maria did hold strong&nbsp;
views and wrote to her brother:

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:17.280
“All, however, are perplexed and&nbsp;
appalled by its dreadful effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.000
and with time the bewilderment&nbsp;
increases rather than diminishes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.800
For my part I recommended you to God,&nbsp;
Who alone can judge you morally.”

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:30.120
After the war, Fermi opposed&nbsp;
working on the hydrogen bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.600
which is orders of magnitude more&nbsp;
powerful than the atomic bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:38.040
on moral and technical grounds…though&nbsp;
he still contributed as a consultant.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:40.860
The detonation of an atomic bomb by the Soviet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.540
Union in 1949 sparked Washington’s&nbsp;
interest in a more powerful weapon.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:50.340
Oppenheimer strongly resisted working&nbsp;
on the hydrogen bomb and faced hearings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:53.760
that questioned his loyalty,&nbsp;
suspecting him to be a Soviet spy.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.180
Despite Fermi and other&nbsp;
scientists coming to his defense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:00.240
Oppenheimer had his security clearance revoked.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.360
Fermi went on to become a distinguished professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.740
of physics at the University of Chicago,&nbsp;
where he was regarded as a superb teacher.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:12.540
During his later years, he raised an interesting&nbsp;
question while lunching with colleagues:

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:13.560
“Where is everybody?”

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:18.240
He was asking why no extraterrestrial&nbsp;
civilizations had been discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.940
despite the great size and age of the universe.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:26.580
This is referred to as a Fermi Paradox because&nbsp;
it’s an apparent contradiction between the high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:31.140
probability of the existence of aliens due to the&nbsp;
vast number of stars that can potentially host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:37.320
habitable planets and the lack of human contact&nbsp;
with such civilizations or evidence for them.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:41.160
Fermi’s view was that if intelligent&nbsp;
life existed elsewhere in the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.680
we should have been visited by them long ago.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.120
While Fermi contemplated the vastness&nbsp;
of the universe, a more immediate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:51.360
personal concern soon cast a shadow over his life.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.520
His health had started to decline.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.080
In the summer of 1954, he was noticeably fatigued.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.560
He visited the doctor in that&nbsp;
fall and they discovered he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.020
had stomach cancer that had metastasized.

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:06.480
He was given six months to live.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.280
Throughout his career, Fermi was&nbsp;
exposed to radioactive materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:14.340
which may have contributed to&nbsp;
the development of his cancer.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:20.940
Fermi was not a religious person, but as he lay&nbsp;
ill and dying, he received visits from priests.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:25.800
His Manhattan Project colleague Leona Libby&nbsp;
who frequently went to see him recalled:

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:30.240
“He spoke of his approaching death as a&nbsp;
great experience, but he asked wistfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:34.560
if I thought there was anything valid in the&nbsp;
idea of an afterlife. He was really cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:39.900
about dying. I came out after each visit and&nbsp;
drove home with tears streaming down my face.”

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:45.120
His condition deteriorated rapidly.
Two months after his diagnosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.340
on November 28, 1954, Enrico&nbsp;
Fermi passed away at his home.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.500
He was 53 years old.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:57.900
In an obituary, the New York Times wrote:&nbsp;
“More than any other man of his time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:02.280
Enrico Fermi could properly be named&nbsp;
"the father of the atomic bomb."

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:07.380
Fermi’s contributions touch our&nbsp;
lives beyond the realm of physics.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:10.200
Tech companies are known to ask candidates complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:14.580
problems during job interviews to see if&nbsp;
they can emulate Fermi’s way of thinking.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:17.100
An example of a “Fermi problem” would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.100
estimating the number of piano&nbsp;
tuners in a city like Toronto.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:23.760
Toronto has a population of&nbsp;
approximately 3 million people.

